{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Give me a burial-hold among you.</b> The custom was that each family had its own burial field to itself, and they had one field where they would bury strangers. Therefore Abraham said, 'I am a stranger and a sojourner with you' — that is, I am a stranger from another land who has come to settle among you, and I have no burial-place that would be an ancestral inheritance; and you — give me a burial-hold among you, so that I may be as one of you even though I am a stranger, since I wish to settle among you. And they answered him: 'Hear us, my lord.' He said 'Hear us' in the plural and 'my lord' in the singular, for one individual was speaking with Abraham on behalf of all of them, and out of respect for them he did not wish to say 'our lord' but said 'my lord' by way of courtesy. And he said, 'You are a prince of Elohim in our midst' — that is, you are not regarded in our eyes as a stranger, but you are in our midst a prince of Elohim; for a stranger who comes from outside is despised, but you are in our midst — honored and a prince like a king, and we and our land are servants to you. Therefore choose whichever burial field you wish, and there bury your dead, for no man among us will withhold from you even his own designated grave. And he answered them: 'If it be your soul' — that is, my desire is not to bury my dead in another's burial field, but if you wish to do me a kindness, entreat Ephron son of Zohar. Thus explained the Ramban."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:23.031053027Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}